<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Günzburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Günzburg is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia-bavaria">Bavarian Swabia</a> at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunz">Günz</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, on the site of the Roman fort of Guntia. It was the chief town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> Margraviate of Burgau, residence of Margrave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-margrave-of-burgau">Charles of Burgau</a> from 1609, and in the 18th century housed an important Austrian mint. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1805.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gunzburg.jpg" alt="Günzburg" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: wuppertaler · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GER_G%C3%BCnzburg,_Marktplatz_005.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Günzburg was founded in about 70 BCE by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> to defend their lands along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and was known as Castellum Guntia, Gontia or Contia, a name taken from the Celtic goddess Gontia. It consisted of a fort, later replaced by at least one other on the same site, a fairly large civilian settlement and most likely an important bridge over the Danube. After the Romans left in the fifth century the Alamanni settled there. The town itself first appears in documents in 1065 as Gunceburch.</p><p>In 1301 the town passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a> and was developed as the centre of the Margraviate of Burgau. From 1803 to 1805 it was for a time the capital of all Further Austria. In 1525, very near the town, the Swabian army defeated the Leipheim Horde in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">German Peasants' War</a>.</p><p>In 1770 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a> stopped there for two days on her journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. On 9 October 1805 troops of the Sixth Corps of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s Grande Armée stormed the Austrian positions in Günzburg and took about a thousand prisoners and six guns. Through the Franco-Bavarian alliance the town was then integrated into the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. In April 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> bombed it, damaging the castle and a munitions train in the station.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Günzburg has about 20,350 inhabitants and is the capital of the Swabian district of Günzburg, formed in 1972. Its sights include the Church of Our Lady, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominikus-zimmermann">Dominikus Zimmermann</a>, the margraves' castle, which is the only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> castle built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the Reisensburg fort, now the congress centre of the University of Ulm, and a nearly intact old town centre. Legoland Deutschland Resort opened nearby in 2002.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Günzburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1609 <b>Günzburg</b> was the residence of Margrave Charles of Burgau, son of Archduke Ferdinand II and Philippine Welser.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-burgau">Margraviate of Burgau</a></li></ul></section>
